- [acc-beliefs-070] Output-Based Verification in Ground-Truth Independent Domains: False Dichotomy — The claim frames the choice as binary—internal inspection vs. output-based verification—ignoring hybrid approaches that combine mechanistic interpretability research with behavioral testing.
- [acc-beliefs-023] Training AI Costs About as Much Energy as Raising a Human: False Equivalence — The claim treats thermodynamic energy quantity as the sole relevant dimension of comparison, ignoring categorical differences in carbon intensity, resource extraction for hardware, water consumption, and waste heat between biological metabolism and data center operation.
- [acc-beliefs-023] Training AI Costs About as Much Energy as Raising a Human: Cherry Picking — The comparison typically selects a single flagship training run and a single human lifetime, ignoring the aggregate energy of the global AI training ecosystem, inference workloads, and hardware supply chains, which break the symmetry the analogy claims.
- [acc-beliefs-023] Training AI Costs About as Much Energy as Raising a Human: Misleading Analogy — Human metabolic energy is drawn from biological food chains with distributed environmental impact, while AI training is concentrated industrial consumption typically powered by fossil-fuel-heavy grids, making the analogy structurally misleading even if the raw joule counts were identical.
- [acc-beliefs-067] Decentralized Markets and Open Development Outperform Central Control in Driving AI Progress: Nirvana Fallacy — The claim implicitly compares real-world centralized planning (with its genuine inefficiencies) against an idealized decentralized market rather than against actually-existing market structures, which themselves exhibit oligopolistic concentration in AI.
- [acc-beliefs-067] Decentralized Markets and Open Development Outperform Central Control in Driving AI Progress: Hasty Generalization — Extrapolating from open-source successes in software (Linux, Apache) to structural superiority across all AI development contexts — including safety-critical frontier research — is a generalization unsupported by systematic cross-domain evidence.
- [acc-beliefs-034] AI Accountability Has a Chicken-and-Egg Problem: False Dilemma — The node concludes that only pre-existing state technical assistance programs or top-down mandatory standards can resolve the bootstrap problem, excluding a range of intermediate or hybrid solutions such as philanthropic pre-funding, academic partnerships, or reformed procurement timelines.
- [acc-beliefs-019] Foundation Model Capabilities Can Be Systematically Unlocked and Deployed: Composition fallacy — The node infers that because capabilities can be unlocked in specific, targeted settings, they can therefore be systematically deployed across all of civilizational infrastructure — scaling a property of parts to an unvalidated whole.
- [acc-beliefs-072] Courts Lack the Expertise to Weigh AI's Diffuse Benefits Against Its Visible Harms: Nirvana Fallacy — The argument implicitly compares imperfect courts against an idealized alternative institution (specialized agencies or legislatures) without rigorously accounting for the competence failures, capture risks, and democratic deficits of those alternatives.
- [acc-beliefs-043] The Biggest Barriers to AI Progress Are Governance Failures, Not Technical Limits: False Dilemma — The node frames the barrier to AI progress as governance failures versus technical limits, implying these are mutually exclusive primary causes and obscuring that both can operate simultaneously as significant constraints.

